---
import GuideLayout from "../../layouts/GuideLayout.astro";
import Card from "../../components/site/Card.astro";
import {
  CLASS_LIMITS,
  FAULT_CLASSES,
  TYPICAL_CONSUMER_CLASS,
  megapixelsFrom,
} from "../../lib/iso";

/**
 * Every number on this page is derived from lib/iso.ts. Nothing is retyped —
 * if the class table there ever changes, this guide changes with it.
 */
const EXAMPLES = [
  { label: "1920×1080", w: 1920, h: 1080 },
  { label: "3840×2160", w: 3840, h: 2160 },
].map((r) => {
  const mp = megapixelsFrom(r.w, r.h);
  const limits = CLASS_LIMITS[TYPICAL_CONSUMER_CLASS];
  return {
    ...r,
    mp,
    // A panel passes while faults-per-million stays within the limit, so the
    // largest whole fault count that still passes is floor(limit × megapixels).
    type1: Math.floor(limits.type1 * mp),
    type2: Math.floor(limits.type2 * mp),
    type3: Math.floor(limits.type3 * mp),
  };
});

const consumer = CLASS_LIMITS[TYPICAL_CONSUMER_CLASS];
const hd = EXAMPLES[0]!;
const uhd = EXAMPLES[1]!;
const fmt = (n: number) => n.toFixed(2);
---

<GuideLayout
  title="ISO Pixel Fault Classes Explained — Class I to IV"
  description="What ISO 13406-2 and ISO 9241-3xx pixel fault classes mean, the three fault types, why the limits are counted per million pixels so the same fault count grades differently on 1080p and 4K, and why a computed class is evidence rather than a guarantee."
  heading="ISO pixel fault classes explained"
  lede="The scheme every pixel warranty is written in: three fault types, four classes, and limits counted per million pixels rather than per screen. That last detail is the one that surprises people."
  updated="2026-08-27"
  slug="iso-pixel-fault-classes"
>
  <Card surface="soft" elevation="e1" padding="lg">
    <p class="text-body-sm font-medium text-ink">In short</p>
    <p class="mt-2 text-body-sm text-body">
      Faults are graded in three types &mdash; permanently lit, permanently dark,
      and stuck sub-pixel &mdash; against four classes, with allowances stated
      <strong>per million pixels</strong>. Because of that, a higher-resolution
      panel is permitted proportionally more faults. Most consumer panels are sold
      against <strong>Class {TYPICAL_CONSUMER_CLASS}</strong>. A computed class is
      evidence for a claim, never a guarantee of one.
    </p>
  </Card>

  <h2>Which standard, and why two names</h2>
  <p>
    The class numbering everyone quotes comes from <strong>ISO
    13406-2:2001</strong>, which has been <strong>withdrawn</strong>. It was
    superseded by ISO 9241-302, -303, -305 and -307:2008. The fault-class scheme
    carried over into the newer documents, and manufacturers still write their
    policies in the 13406-2 class numbering in practice &mdash; which is why both
    names appear here and in the report. If your warranty document cites the
    withdrawn standard, that is normal and it does not make the document invalid.
  </p>

  <h2>The three fault types</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Type 1 &mdash; permanently lit.</strong> A pixel stuck on, showing
      white on a black field. Often called a hot or bright pixel. Usually the most
      distracting, and usually the type policies allow fewest of.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Type 2 &mdash; permanently dark.</strong> A pixel receiving no
      power, so it stays black even on white. A dead pixel.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Type 3 &mdash; stuck sub-pixel.</strong> One or more of a
      pixel&rsquo;s three channels jammed on or off, so the pixel still lights but
      with the wrong colour. The most common fault by a wide margin, and the one
      allowed in the largest numbers.
    </li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    If you are not sure which you are looking at, read
    <a href="/guides/dead-vs-stuck-pixel">dead versus stuck pixel</a> first &mdash;
    the type determines the allowance, so misclassifying a fault changes the answer.
  </p>

  <h2>The class table</h2>
  <div class="guide-table">
    <table>
      <caption>
        Maximum permitted faults per million pixels. Class I permits none of
        anything.
      </caption>
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th scope="col">Class</th>
          <th scope="col">Type 1</th>
          <th scope="col">Type 2</th>
          <th scope="col">Type 3</th>
          <th scope="col">Clusters (type 1/2)</th>
          <th scope="col">Clusters (type 3)</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        {
          FAULT_CLASSES.map((c) => (
            <tr>
              <th scope="row">
                Class {c}
                {c === TYPICAL_CONSUMER_CLASS && " (typical consumer)"}
              </th>
              <td>{CLASS_LIMITS[c].type1}</td>
              <td>{CLASS_LIMITS[c].type2}</td>
              <td>{CLASS_LIMITS[c].type3}</td>
              <td>{CLASS_LIMITS[c].clusterFull}</td>
              <td>{CLASS_LIMITS[c].clusterSub}</td>
            </tr>
          ))
        }
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <p>
    Class I is a zero-defect panel and is rare and expensive. Class IV is
    extremely permissive and you are unlikely to be offered anything graded
    against it. Nearly everything sold to consumers sits at Class
    {TYPICAL_CONSUMER_CLASS} &mdash; useful context, but not a promise about any
    specific product, and not a substitute for reading the policy that came with
    yours.
  </p>

  <h2>Why resolution changes the answer</h2>
  <p>
    The limits above are stated <strong>per million pixels</strong>, not per
    screen. So the same absolute fault count grades differently depending on how
    many pixels the panel has. A {hd.label} panel is {fmt(hd.mp)}&nbsp;MP; a
    {" "}{uhd.label} panel is {fmt(uhd.mp)}&nbsp;MP &mdash; four times as many
    pixels in the same physical area, and therefore four times the allowance.
  </p>
  <p>
    Working Class {TYPICAL_CONSUMER_CLASS} through both, using its type&nbsp;1
    limit of {consumer.type1} per million:
  </p>
  <div class="guide-table">
    <table>
      <caption>
        Largest whole fault count still within Class {TYPICAL_CONSUMER_CLASS},
        computed as the per-million limit multiplied by the panel&rsquo;s
        megapixels.
      </caption>
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th scope="col">Panel</th>
          <th scope="col">Megapixels</th>
          <th scope="col">Type 1 allowed</th>
          <th scope="col">Type 2 allowed</th>
          <th scope="col">Type 3 allowed</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        {
          EXAMPLES.map((e) => (
            <tr>
              <th scope="row">{e.label}</th>
              <td>{fmt(e.mp)} MP</td>
              <td>{e.type1}</td>
              <td>{e.type2}</td>
              <td>{e.type3}</td>
            </tr>
          ))
        }
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <p>
    Read that carefully, because it is counter-intuitive: {uhd.type1} bright
    pixels on a {uhd.label} panel is within Class {TYPICAL_CONSUMER_CLASS}, while
    the same {uhd.type1} on {hd.label} is well outside it &mdash; the 1080p panel
    only tolerates {hd.type1}. The faults are equally visible to you in both
    cases. The standard is counting defect <em>density</em>, not annoyance.
  </p>
  <p>
    One practical trap follows from this. The megapixel figure has to come from
    physical device pixels, not the CSS pixels a browser reports. A 4K display
    presenting as {hd.label} at a device pixel ratio of 2 really has
    {" "}{fmt(uhd.mp)}&nbsp;MP, and using the CSS figure would roughly quadruple
    the calculated fault density and wrongly fail a perfectly good panel. The
    {" "}<a href="/report">report</a> works from device pixels for exactly this
    reason.
  </p>

  <h2>Clusters</h2>
  <p>
    ISO also limits <strong>clusters</strong> &mdash; several faults falling
    within a 5&times;5 pixel block, which read as one visible blemish rather than
    several separate dots. Note that Classes I to III permit no clusters of
    type&nbsp;1 or type&nbsp;2 faults at all.
  </p>
  <p>
    This site reports clusters as <strong>possible only</strong>, and never uses
    one to fail a class. The reason is a straightforward accuracy limit: we know
    where you tapped, and a fingertip or a cursor carries error far larger than
    five pixels. Flagging a possible cluster tells you to look again closely at
    that spot with your own eyes. It is not an ISO cluster determination, and we
    will not pretend otherwise.
  </p>

  <h2>The honest caveat</h2>
  <p>
    These classes are <strong>guidelines, not mandates</strong>. Nothing compels a
    manufacturer to adopt them, they are interpreted inconsistently across the
    industry, and some manufacturers ignore the scheme entirely in favour of their
    own thresholds. A computed class is <strong>evidence</strong> you can put in
    front of a support agent &mdash; a defensible count of faults by type,
    normalised the way the industry normalises them. It is not a guarantee of a
    remedy. What actually governs your claim is the manufacturer&rsquo;s own
    published policy for your exact model, plus the retailer&rsquo;s returns
    policy and your statutory rights. See
    <a href="/guides/dead-pixel-warranty-policies">how to find the policy that
    applies</a>.
  </p>

  <h2>Source</h2>
  <p>
    The class table in this site&rsquo;s code is transcribed from
    {" "}<a
      href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/ISO_13406-2"
      rel="noopener"
      target="_blank">the Wikipedia article on ISO 13406-2</a
    >, verified 2026-08-27. That is a <strong>secondary source</strong>, and we
    say so rather than implying we have read the standard: ISO 13406-2 and the
    9241 series are paywalled documents sold by ISO and its national member
    bodies. If a claim turns on an exact figure, buy the current standard or ask
    the manufacturer to cite the clause they are relying on. To grade your own
    panel, run the <a href="/test/sequence">guided pixel test</a> and open your
    {" "}<a href="/report">report</a>.
  </p>
</GuideLayout>
